2026-10-30","vl":"Spanish Guinea"},{"qid":"Q983","kind":"flag","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Flag of Equatorial Guinea.svg","from_t":1979.6356164383562,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P41","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q983","kind":"coat","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Coat of arms of Equatorial Guinea.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P94","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q983","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q211936","value_label":"Francisco Macías Nguema Biyoko","from_t":1968.77868852459,"to_t":1979.5863013698631,"title":"President of Equatorial Guinea","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Francisco Macías Nguema Biyoko"},{"qid":"Q983","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q170554","value_label":"Flag of Equatorial Guinea.svg","from_t":1979.6356164383562,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Equatorial Guinea","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Equatorial Guinea"}],"people":{"Q57385":{"label":"Teodoro Obiang","description":"Equatoguinean politician, President of Equatorial Guinea since 1979","enwiki":"Teodoro Obiang Nguema Mbasogo","summary":"Teodoro Obiang Nguema Mbasogo (born 5 June 1942) is an Equatoguinean politician, former military officer, and dictator who has led Equatorial Guinea since 1979, including as its second president since 1982. As of 2026, he is the second-longest-consecutively-serving current non-royal national leader in the world, second to Paul Biya of Cameroon.\nAfter graduating from military school in Zaragoza, Spain, Obiang held multiple positions under the presidency of his uncle, Francisco Macías Nguema, including director of the Black Beach prison notorious for its human rights violations. He ousted Macías in a military coup in 1979 and took control of the country as president and chairman of the Supreme Military Council.","image":"2026 Teodoro Obiang (cropped).jpg","birth":1942.4246575342465,"death":null},"Q129263796":{"label":"Manuel Osa Nsue Nsua","description":"Equatoguinean politician","enwiki":"Manuel Osa Nsue Nsua","summary":"Manuel Osa Nsue Nsua (born 21 July 1976) is an Equatoguinean politician and banker who has served as the prime minister of Equatorial Guinea since August 2024. He previously served as the head of the national bank from 2012 until his appointment as prime minister.","image":"Manuel Osa Nsue Nsua (cropped).jpg","birth":1976.551912568306,"death":null},"Q211936":{"label":"Francisco Macías Nguema Biyoko","description":"Equatoguinean politician, 1st and former President of Equatorial Guinea (1924-1979)","enwiki":"Francisco Macías Nguema","summary":"Francisco Macías Nguema (born Mez-m Ngueme, later Africanised to Masie Nguema Biyogo Ñegue Ndong; 1 January 1924 – 29 September 1979), often referred to as Macías Nguema or simply Macías, was an Equatoguinean politician who served as the first president of Equatorial Guinea from the country's gaining of independence in 1968, until his overthrow in 1979. He is widely remembered as one of the most brutal dictators in history. As president, he exhibited bizarre and erratic behavior, to the point that many of his contemporaries believed he was insane.\nA member of the Fang people, Macías Nguema held numerous official positions under Spanish colonial rule before being elected the first president of the soon-to-be independent country in 1968.","image":"Don Francisco Macias.jpg","birth":1924,"death":1979.7424657534248}},"media":{"Flag of Equatorial Guinea.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Flag_of_Equatorial_Guinea.svg/330px-Flag_of_Equatorial_Guinea.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Equatorial_Guinea.svg","author":"See File history below for details.","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Equatorial Guinea.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Coat_of_arms_of_Equatorial_Guinea.svg/330px-Coat_of_